"""
ecomode-max - keep an HP OMEN's EC in the performance profile.

Why this exists
---------------
On HP OMEN 16-wf0 (board 8BAB) the embedded controller keeps a
user-selected thermal profile for only 120 s after the last WMI
0x20008/0x10 query, then falls back to its own ~44 W limits over PECI.
RAPL keeps reading 130 W, so nothing in Linux shows the drop. hp-wmi on
6.18 sends 0x10 only when platform_profile is written - but on every write,
even of the same value. Rewriting the current profile every 30 s therefore
holds the full limits. Measurements are in the README.

Session-scoped by construction
------------------------------
`on` starts a transient systemd unit bound to the invoking user's manager
(BindsTo=user@UID.service). With lingering on, that manager outlives the
logout, so the keeper also asks logind every round whether the user is still
logged in. Performance is held through a power-profiles-daemon profile hold,
which the daemon never saves and drops by itself when the holder goes away.
Nothing here survives a logout or a reboot.

No password
-----------
The polkit action io.github.souloriginal.powermodes.max lets the active local session run
this file without a password. That covers everything it does:
- start and stop Max, with the fans on auto, max (full speed) or boost (full
  speed while the CPU package is hot), and switch between them;
- put the fans back to automatic or to full speed by hand (`fans`), with
  Max off;
- when Max starts over Eco, roll Eco back to the originals it recorded
  (`ecomode off`, whose exact restore this reuses);
- start and stop nvidia-powerd (NVIDIA Dynamic Boost) along with Max, if it
  was not running already: it lifts the GPU's limit from 80 W to about
  130 W in the performance profile (measured);
- start and stop the root watts sampler, which publishes an averaged
  package wattage, what limits the CPU right now (MSR 0x64F, read-only) and
  - while some other process keeps the dGPU awake anyway - its load, clocks
  and draw;
- with `--gpu-profile`, select a LACT profile for the dGPU while Max runs
  (an overclock the user defined in LACT) and put the previous one back
  when Max ends. The name is checked against LACT's own list.
run, cleanup and watts-run start under systemd only, and every argument is
checked: there are no free-form paths or values.
"""

